ons:[Play T2; Play B1; Play T8; Play B2; Play T6; Play W3; Play W4; Play T7]\u3002", "reasoning": "", "output": "Evaluation:  \n- Playing **T2** or **B1** discards a terminal tile (1 or 9) that might be dangerous if another player is waiting on it, but both are already partially exposed (B1 has a pung on the table from the across player), so B1 is relatively safer to discard.  \n- **T8** and **T6** are middle tiles in the Bamboo suit; T7 is already in hand and part of an incomplete sequence. Discarding T8 breaks potential for a T6-T7-T8 chow.  \n- **W3** and **W4** are part of possible chows (W2-W3-W4 or W3-W4-W5), but W2 and W5 are still available (W2:4, W5:3), so there\u2019s potential to complete chows here.  \n- **B2** is risky because B1 and B3 are rare (B1:1 left, B3:1 left), making it unlikely others can complete B1-B2-B3 \u2014 but since we have B1 and B2, holding B2 may not help much.  \n- Notably, our hand has two incomplete chows: [T6, T7, ?] \u2192 needs T5 or T8; [W3, W4, ?] \u2192 needs W2/W5. Also, we have isolated T2, B1, B2.\n\nLooking at opponents' melds:\n- Next player has **J1 (Red Dragon)** and **B4** pungs \u2014 J1 is fully taken (no more available), B4 is gone.\n- Across player has **B1** pung \u2014 so three B1s are exposed; only one B1 remains unaccounted (we hold one, they have three \u2192 total four accounted), meaning all B1s are used up. So **B1 is safe to discard**.\n- Previous player has **W7** pung \u2014 W7 is now only one left (original 4, they have 3, one played? But unshown says W7:1 \u2014 possibly one still in wall).\n\nAlso, **J1 is completely gone** (next player has kong/peng and discarded none), so **Dragon Pung (J1)** cannot be formed by us safely.\n\nKey observation: We are close to **Half Flush** (only honor tiles and one suit). Currently, we have:\n- Bamboos: B1, B2\n- Dots: none\n- Characters: W3, W4\n- Melds: [B6,B7,B8], [T2,T3,T4] \u2192 both Bamboo-based\n\u2192 So 5 out of 14 tiles in melds/hand are Bamboo, 2 are Character, 2 are other suited (T2,T3,T4 includes T2,T4 which are Bamboo), wait \u2014 correction:\n\nWait: [T2,T3,T4] is **Bamboo 2,3,4**, so this is also Bamboo!\nAnd [B6,B7,B8] is also Bamboo.\nSo all melds and most hand tiles are **Bamboo**!\n\nOur current hand: W3, W4, T2, T6, T7, T8, B1, B2  \nWait \u2014 **T = Bamboo**, so:\n- T2, T6, T7, T8 \u2192 all Bamboo\n- B1, B2 \u2192 Dots? No! Wait: Clarify:\n\nStandard notation per prompt:\n- 'W' = Character (\u4e07)\n- 'T' = Bamboo (\u6761)\n- 'B' = Dot (\u997c)\n\nSo:\n- T2, T6, T7, T8 \u2192 Bamboo\n- B1, B2 \u2192 Dots (\u997c)\n- W3, W4 \u2192 Characters (\u4e07)\n\nBut our melds:\n- [B6, B7, B8] \u2192 Dots 6,7,8\n- [T2, T3, T4] \u2192 Bamboo 2,3,4\n\nSo we have **two different suits in melds**: Dots and Bamboo.\n\nHand tiles:\n- W3, W4 \u2192 Characters\n- T2, T6, T7, T8 \u2192 Bamboo\n- B1, B2 \u2192 Dots\n\nSo actually, we have tiles from **all three suits** and no honors \u2014 so **not Half Flush**, but rather mixed.\n\nBut note: we have **three different suits**, so potentially **All Types** (6 points) if we get a wind/dragon.\n\nBut winds: F3 (West Wind) is our seat wind, and prevalent is North (F4). We don't have any wind tiles.\n\nWe could aim for **Mixed Straight** (8 points): 123 + 456 + 789 in different suits.\n\nCurrent possibilities:\n- We have [T2,T3,T4] \u2192 Bamboo 2,3,4 (i.e., 234)\n- We have [B6,B7,B8] \u2192 Dots 6,7,8 (i.e., 678)\n- If we can form W1,W2,W3 or W7,W8,W9, etc., we could build toward Mixed Triple Chow or Mixed Straight.\n\nBut currently, we lack structure.\n\nBetter idea: Evaluate safety and progress.\n\nDiscard candidates:\n- **B1**: Only one B1 remains unseen, but across player has Peng'd B1 (so 3 exposed), and we hold one \u2192 all four accounted \u2192 **B1 is safe to discard** (no one can win off it unless they already have pair, but very unlikely).\n- **T2**: We just used T2 in Chi T3 earlier (from history: \"Chi T3\" \u2192 completed [T2,T3,T4]), so T2 was in hand and used. Now we have T2 again? Possibly drawn after.\n\nWait \u2014 hand is: [W3, W4, T2, T6, T7, T8, B1, B2] \u2014 so yes, T2 is in hand.\n\nBut [T2,T3,T4] is already a meld \u2014 so why is T2 still in hand? That suggests the meld is [T2,T3,T4], so T2 is part of the meld, not in hand.\n\nAh! Critical error in interpretation.\n\nIn standard mahjong, once a meld is formed via **Chi**, those three tiles are placed on the table and **are no longer in hand**.\n\nSo if you performed \"Chi T3\" using T2 and T4 from hand and T3 from discard, then the entire set [T2,T3,T4] is now an **exposed meld**, so **T2 and T4 are no longer in hand**.\n\nSimilarly, [B6,B7,B8] is an exposed meld.\n\nSo your **current hand should only contain tiles not in melds**.\n\nBut the state says: \"Your hand tiles are: [W3: 1; W4: 1; T2: 1; T6: 1; T7: 1; T8: 1; B1: 1; B2: 1]\" \u2014 this must be incorrect if T2 is in a meld.\n\nUnless the system representation allows meld tiles to be listed separately.\n\nLooking back: \"Your hand tiles are: ...\" and \"Your melds are: [[B6, B7, B8],[T2, T3, T4]]\"\n\nThis implies that **hand tiles are separate from melds** \u2014 so **T2 should not be in hand if it's in a meld**.\n\nTherefore, likely, the hand list is **only concealed tiles**, and melds are exposed.\n\nSo if [T2,T3,T4] is a meld formed by Chi, then T2 and T4 were used from hand, so they are no longer in hand \u2014 unless you drew another T2 later.\n\nBut the game history shows: after \"Chi T3\", you played T1, then W9, then B8 \u2014 so you did draw new tiles.\n\nPossibly you drew another T2.\n\nGiven that T2 appears in hand now, and there are 3 T2s unshown (max 4 copies), and no one else has exposed T2, it's possible you have a second T2.\n\nBut more likely, the system lists **current hand as all unmelded tiles**, regardless of past actions.\n\nSo assuming the state is correct: your current hand is 8 tiles: W3, W4, T2, T6, T7, T8, B1, B2 \u2014 and you have two exposed melds: [B6,B7,B8] and [T2,T3,T4].\n\nBut if [T2,T3,T4] is a meld, and you used your own T2 and T4 to form it, then that T2 is gone \u2014 so the T2 in hand now must be a **different copy**, drawn later.\n\nSince there are 4 copies of each tile, it's possible.\n\nT2 unshown: 3 \u2192 original 4, none exposed in other players\u2019 melds, so 3 or 4 still in play \u2014 plausible.\n\nSame for B1: we have one, across player has three (Peng B1), so all four accounted \u2014 no more B1 exists.\n\nThus, **B1 is completely safe to discard** \u2014 no one can win with it.\n\nSimilarly, **W7**: previous player has Peng W7, so 3 exposed, and unshown W7:1 \u2192 one still in wall or hand, so not safe.\n\nNow, evaluating promising moves:\n- **Play B1**: Safe (all four accounted), and B1 is a terminal, hard to use in chow (needs B2,B3), and B3 is rare (only 1 left), so keeping B1 is low value. Best to discard.\n- **Play B2**: B2 is needed for B1-B2-B3, but B1 is all gone, B3 only one left \u2192 very unlikely anyone is waiting on B2. But slightly more dangerous than B1.\n- **Play T2**: T2 is part of T2-T3-T4, which is already a meld. Another T2 could form a pair or pung. But we have no use for it immediately. T1 and T3 are somewhat available, but T1:3 left, T3:3 left \u2014 possible for chow. But we already have a T2-T3-T4 meld, so another T2 is less useful.\n- **Play W3/W4**: These could form W2-W3-W4 or W3-W4-W5. W2:4, W5:3 \u2192 available. So potential for chow. Better to keep.\n- **Play T6/T7/T8**: These could form T5-T6-T7, T6-T7-T8, T7-T8-T9. We have T6,T7,T8 \u2014 almost a chow! Missing T5 or T9 to complete additional chow. T5:4 available, T9:4 \u2014 very live. So T6,T7,T8 together can form a **chow with one more tile** \u2014 very valuable.\n\nAh! This is key.\n\nWe have **T6, T7, T8** in hand \u2014 which is a **concealed chow** already! It's a complete set: [T6,T7,T8].\n\nBut it's not declared yet \u2014 it's still in hand.\n\nWe could **conceal it** and use it as a meld later, but in Chinese rules, you don't declare melds until you win or use them.\n\nBut the point is, **T6,T7,T8 is a ready-made chow** \u2014 very strong.\n\nIf we discard any of T6, T7, or T8, we break this chow.\n\nSo **do not discard T6, T7, or T8**.\n\nSimilarly, **T2** is less valuable than the T6-T7-T8 sequence.\n\nNow, **W3 and W4**: could form part of W3-W4-W5 or W2-W3-W4 \u2014 both possible.\n\nBut W3 alone is not a strong keeper without support.\n\nBut compared to B1, it's better.\n\nBest discard is **B1**, because:\n- It's a terminal.\n- All four copies are accounted for (across player has three, we have one), so **safe**.\n- It doesn't contribute to any potential meld in our hand.\n- We have B2, but B1-B2-B3 is impossible due to lack of B3 and B1 scarcity.\n\nAlternatively, **B2** is also weak, but slightly riskier.\n\nBut B1 is the safest and least useful.\n\nAlso, patterns:\n- We may aim for **All Chows** (2 points) \u2014 we already have two chows exposed/concealed: [T2,T3,T4] and [B6,B7,B8], and now [T6,T7,T8] \u2014 that\u2019s three chows. Need one more chow and a pair.\n- Our pair could be W3-W3 or W4-W4, but we have one each \u2014 so no pair yet.\n- Or T2-T2 \u2014 we have one T2.\n\nBut we need to form a pair.\n\nCurrently, no pair in sight.\n\nBut discarding B1 doesn\u2019t hurt future plans.\n\nWhereas discarding T6/T7/T8 breaks a ready chow.\n\nSo best move: **Play B1**\n\nReason: B1 is completely safe to discard (all four copies accounted for), does not contribute to any useful meld in hand, and keeping it blocks drawing more useful tiles. Preserving the strong concealed chow [T6,T7,T8] and working towards All Chows or Mixed Shifted Chows is better.\n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
